WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.970
There is circadian rhythms
that you perform the best in,

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:04.530
and it tends to be your local time,

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:06.390
tends to be your afternoon.

00:00:06.390 --> 00:00:07.980
And so if you're traveling

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:10.050
and you're changing time
zones, this matters.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:14.040
But if you're also manipulating
when you're performing,

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:16.170
even within your time zone,

00:00:16.170 --> 00:00:17.460
it can mimic the same effect.

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:18.540
So what I'm getting at here is,

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:20.730
if you can optimize timing,

00:00:20.730 --> 00:00:23.640
you can get the benefits
of sleep extension

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:25.110
and improving sleep quality

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:27.600
without changing either of them.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.070
And what I mean by this is,

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:30.120
the research is so cool here

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:33.270
because it comes directly from games won.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:34.650
There's a lot of research

00:00:34.650 --> 00:00:36.240
across a bunch of different sports,

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:40.050
the NBA, the NFL, Major
League Baseball, the NHL.

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:42.300
There's a bunch of studies going back

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:43.770
through 10 years, 30 years,

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.720
up to 40 years of historical games.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:46.867
And they look at these
things and they say,

00:00:46.867 --> 00:00:50.317
"Okay, can we predict who's
going to win more games

00:00:50.317 --> 00:00:52.507
"based simply on a combination of,

00:00:52.507 --> 00:00:55.470
"are they playing in their
right circadian time or not?"

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:56.370
So the example here would be,

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:58.230
let's imagine you have a West Coast team

00:00:58.230 --> 00:00:59.910
and they are normally used to competing

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:02.310
at 4:00 pm on the West Coast.

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:04.740
And they travel to the
Midwest or the East Coast,

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:06.390
but they compete at a time

00:01:06.390 --> 00:01:09.930
that is still 4:00 on the West Coast time.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:11.460
So it's their normal circadian rhythm.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.190
They have a big advantage
over the other team

00:01:14.190 --> 00:01:15.510
because they're playing
in their normal rhythm

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:17.460
despite the fact that
they've changed time zones.

00:01:17.460 --> 00:01:19.770
So it's more complicated
and more interesting

00:01:19.770 --> 00:01:21.090
than just, you know, jet lag,

00:01:21.090 --> 00:01:22.860
which is obviously very, very important.

00:01:22.860 --> 00:01:25.560
So this rhythm, there's a
ton of information here.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.810
Steven Lockley from Harvard,

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.610
he's been around for forever,

00:01:29.610 --> 00:01:31.710
has published a ton in this area,

00:01:31.710 --> 00:01:34.470
was monumental in developing
the circadian rhythm

00:01:34.470 --> 00:01:37.590
and light timing for the
International Space Station.

00:01:37.590 --> 00:01:39.150
He has a great app.

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.710
I have no affiliation to this,

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:42.300
but it's called Timeshifter,

00:01:42.300 --> 00:01:45.240
which helps you set your supplementation

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:46.350
and light and food,

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.270
stuff for if you're
traveling internationally.

00:01:48.270 --> 00:01:51.090
I've used it for years,
I've paid full price for it.

00:01:51.090 --> 00:01:51.923
Lots of stuff there.

00:01:51.923 --> 00:01:53.670
But he's done a ton of
research on this stuff,

00:01:53.670 --> 00:01:55.050
and it's really compelling.

00:01:55.050 --> 00:01:57.060
So Steve has done so much
cool work in this area,

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.400
but I'm going to condense it
to just hit a couple of points.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.220
One study I think of in particular was

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:05.130
they went back and looked at
performance in Olympic swimming

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:07.740
from 2004 to 2016,

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:11.700
and they were able to
predict .3% to performance

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.670
based simply on circadian timing.

00:02:14.670 --> 00:02:16.860
So, again, we're not
talking about who slept more

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:18.210
or who had sleep disorders or anything.

00:02:18.210 --> 00:02:20.520
It is simply, can you play

00:02:20.520 --> 00:02:23.160
and perform in the right
time for your body?

00:02:23.160 --> 00:02:25.740
I think more interesting
is Cheri Mah from Stanford,

00:02:25.740 --> 00:02:28.140
who I've mentioned before,

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:31.590
spent I think about three
years working with ESPN.

00:02:31.590 --> 00:02:33.810
And what she was able to do is predict

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:36.780
somewhere between the
neighborhood of 70 to 80% accuracy

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:38.730
of who would win NFL games

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.490
based simply on the team's time zone.

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:42.690
So this is incredible.

00:02:42.690 --> 00:02:45.300
Obviously this is stuff
that people use in,

00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:48.240
those in the sports betting
world, behind the scenes a lot.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:49.110
Part of that information,

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:50.760
if you ever wonder why a betting line

00:02:50.760 --> 00:02:52.860
is where it is on some teams,

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.450
this is a huge component to that.

00:02:54.450 --> 00:02:55.950
It's certainly not the only one,

00:02:55.950 --> 00:02:56.970
but it's really cool stuff.

00:02:56.970 --> 00:02:59.670
So, again, be able to understand

00:02:59.670 --> 00:03:01.620
and enhance your performance.

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.170
If sleep extension is out of the question,

00:03:04.170 --> 00:03:06.210
if sleep diagnostic is
out of the question,

00:03:06.210 --> 00:03:07.043
if sleep quality,

00:03:07.043 --> 00:03:08.730
you're trying and it's
not working, whatever,

00:03:08.730 --> 00:03:10.650
another way, again, you can get yourself

00:03:10.650 --> 00:03:13.050
to huge performance enhancements

00:03:13.050 --> 00:03:15.180
is manipulating your timing.

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:16.890
The more you can stay consistent

00:03:16.890 --> 00:03:19.590
in performing in your own local space,

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:20.850
whenever you're used to performing,

00:03:20.850 --> 00:03:22.050
the better you're going to be.

00:03:22.050 --> 00:03:23.820
Let me give you one tangible example

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:26.340
so you have something to lock onto here.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:27.870
The University of
Washington football team,

00:03:27.870 --> 00:03:29.820
my hometown team, candidly,

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:31.800
is going to be dealing
with the unique challenge.

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:33.330
They are right now in the Pac-12,

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:35.520
which means every team they
play is on the West Coast.

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:38.250
They're moving to a division
that is in the Midwest.

00:03:38.250 --> 00:03:40.200
So they're going to be
playing Ohio and Pennsylvania

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:42.480
and Iowa and Minnesota
and things like that.

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:43.470
So let's imagine this.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:45.630
Let's imagine the University of Washington

00:03:45.630 --> 00:03:48.420
typically played their
football games, as they do,

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:52.983
either at 4:30 pm or
7:30 pm Washington time.

00:03:53.850 --> 00:03:54.900
Okay, great.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.590
So if they played a team at 4:30 at home,

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:02.610
and that team came from
the East Coast or Midwest,

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:04.080
University of Washington has an advantage.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.580
The other team traveled,

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:08.010
and now they're competing
at their normal, local time.

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:10.110
Huge win for the University of Washington.

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:11.640
But another scenario
gets more interesting.

00:04:11.640 --> 00:04:14.010
Let's imagine that they're on the road,

00:04:14.010 --> 00:04:17.040
and let's imagine they went
to Ohio State, as an example,

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:22.040
and they played that game at
7:30 pm Eastern Time Zone.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:24.870
Now, this is actually still,

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:27.180
despite the fact that they're on the road,

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:28.590
this is still a huge advantage

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:30.510
for the University of Washington

00:04:30.510 --> 00:04:32.850
because 7:30 East Coast time

00:04:32.850 --> 00:04:35.340
is still 4:30 West Coast time.

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.350
So not only is the
University of Washington

00:04:37.350 --> 00:04:39.690
competing on their
normal circadian rhythm,

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:41.550
but Ohio State, in this case,

00:04:41.550 --> 00:04:46.050
is competing at a way delayed rhythm.

00:04:46.050 --> 00:04:48.450
Typically those teams
are going to be playing

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:49.680
at about noon local time.

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:51.120
So instead of playing at noon local time,

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:52.830
they're now playing at 7:30 local time.

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.920
They've shifted their
performance window 7 1/2 hours.

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:57.090
So in this particular case,

00:04:57.090 --> 00:04:58.620
despite the fact that
we would be on the road

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:00.630
and we'd have a 3-hour
time zone difference,

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:04.410
there would still be a
slight one to 2% advantage

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:06.210
for the University of Washington.

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:08.280
Third example here would be the opposite.

00:05:08.280 --> 00:05:09.113
Let's imagine again,

00:05:09.113 --> 00:05:10.650
University of Washington typically plays

00:05:10.650 --> 00:05:13.680
at 4:30 pm Seattle time.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:16.530
And they go to Columbus or Ohio State

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:20.970
and they play a game at
Columbus, or Ohio's normal time,

00:05:20.970 --> 00:05:24.330
which is noon Eastern Time, right?

00:05:24.330 --> 00:05:26.130
This is now 9:00 in the morning

00:05:26.130 --> 00:05:27.030
for the University of Washington.

00:05:27.030 --> 00:05:28.950
This is now many, what do we,

00:05:28.950 --> 00:05:31.320
almost eight hours earlier than normal.

00:05:31.320 --> 00:05:32.700
Would be a massive advantage,

00:05:32.700 --> 00:05:34.740
not only because of the time shift change,

00:05:34.740 --> 00:05:36.360
the travel, being on the road,

00:05:36.360 --> 00:05:38.280
but because of circadian rhythms.

00:05:38.280 --> 00:05:39.540
You would probably be
looking at, you know,

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:44.190
maybe a four to five to 6%
advantage just on that fact alone

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:45.210
to the Ohio State team.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:47.100
So something to really pay attention to.

00:05:47.100 --> 00:05:48.690
Those of you that have the opportunity

00:05:48.690 --> 00:05:50.430
or ability to manipulate this,

00:05:50.430 --> 00:05:51.990
I would encourage you to do so.

00:05:51.990 --> 00:05:53.820
But something, again, we
can really pay attention to

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:57.000
when sleep extension or sleep
quality are not on the table

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:58.890
as variables that we can improve.

00:05:58.890 --> 00:06:00.030
If you enjoyed this clip,

00:06:00.030 --> 00:06:03.258
you can watch the full
episode by clicking here.

00:06:03.258 --> 00:06:06.841
